"""Locating and identifying the identity marker on an OpenPhotoEdit export.

Every export carries a small identity marker: a 3x3 grid of saturated colour
cells stamped into the image.  The grid is not decorative, it is a code.  Each
column drives one channel of the RGB model while the other two are held down,
and each row steps the driven channel through a dark, a mid and a bright
level:

    column 0  red     cell 0 dark  ->  cell 3 mid  ->  cell 6 bright
    column 1  green   cell 1 dark  ->  cell 4 mid  ->  cell 7 bright
    column 2  blue    cell 2 dark  ->  cell 5 mid  ->  cell 8 bright

The marker therefore survives any edit that does not repaint it: its identity
is carried by the *shape* of the grid rather than by absolute colour, so
exposure, contrast, cropping and filter changes still leave a readable code as
long as the nine cells keep their relative ordering.

Detection runs on a finished export, where the marker occupies an unknown
fraction of the frame and everything around it is arbitrary photograph.  The
search therefore scores candidate boxes by how closely the nine cells inside
them reproduce the ramp structure, which is a property of the marker alone and
does not need the surrounding pixels to be known.
"""

